From 95395fb10aee7acfdffbf353709316acc24d6098 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Lamb <paul@paulscode.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 14:39:34 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 4/5] Servers: report the BLAKE2b fork point in server.features

genesis_hash cannot tell the SHA256 and BLAKE2b chains apart: the forked
chain keeps the original's history, genesis included. A client, or a
service picking an Electrum backend for one chain, has had no way to ask
which side a server follows.

When the indexed chain contains an extended header, server.features now
carries

  "blake2b_fork": {"height": <first extended height>, "hash": <its id>,
                   "header_bytes": 164, "block_hash": "blake2b"}

the same shape Electrs Pruned reports, so both can be read alike. The
height is found by binary search over the stored headers
(BTC::FindFirstV2Height), not compiled in, so a regtest activation
height is reported correctly. Storage caches the answer and re-checks it
against the stored header on each call. The key is absent on a chain with
no extended header (the SHA256 chain, or a BLAKE2b index still below the
split), which is itself the answer for that chain.

Only the client-facing server.features carries it; the dict sent to peers
is unchanged.
